{
  "image_name": "838121f3077461d2a4b7ace6f6fae857.png",
  "prompt_text": "The figure summarizes how microorganisms interact with metal surfaces and corrosion products under aerobic and anaerobic conditions. Overall, it shows that microbes can either accelerate metal oxidation by consuming electrons or by transforming sulfur, iron, carbon, and nitrogen compounds, and they can also generate mineral precipitates on the surface.\n\nIn the upper part, iron corrosion is linked to both abiotic chemistry and microbial respiration. Iron oxidizers use oxygen, while anaerobic respirers use nitrate, sulfate, carbon dioxide, fumarate, or similar electron acceptors. Their activity connects dissolved Fe²⁺, sulfide, carbonate, phosphate, and other ions to the formation of iron minerals such as sulfides, carbonates, phosphates, and oxides.\n\nThe larger middle panel places these reactions within a biofilm. Different microbial groups exchange metabolites such as H₂, acetate, organics, methane, sulfate, sulfide, and Fe²⁺/Fe³⁺, creating a network of coupled processes on the metal surface. Iron-oxidizing, iron-reducing, sulfur-oxidizing, sulfate-reducing, methanogenic, and nitrate-reducing organisms all appear to contribute.\n\nThe lower panels compare passivating metals and copper. For Al, Zn, or Ti, an oxide film usually blocks oxygen and proton access, but microbial damage or consumption can undermine protection. For copper, microbes promote either copper oxalate formation in air or copper sulfide precipitation anaerobically.",
